3. bbrv3-2026-experimental-android15-6.6.patch
   Algorithm source: https://github.com/google/bbr
   Branch: bbr-v3-2026-09-16-01
   Commit: 674859761ded9f32690c7bdaf22ef585531452ed
   Path: net/ipv4/tcp_bbr3.c (Git blob d7070bbffed7c9908a09def2c13a3eb7d2fb171b,
   SHA256 7a8b7e6df08f6815b78b7c03ce48af5829215ab6f7227dadfd980c4f7a4538a9)
   SPDX-License-Identifier of that file: GPL-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause (kept in line 1).
   MODULE_AUTHOR entries: Van Jacobson, Neal Cardwell, Yuchung Cheng,
   Soheil Hassas Yeganeh, Priyaranjan Jha, Yousuk Seung, Kevin Yang, Arjun Roy,
   David Morley. MODULE_LICENSE declaration: Dual BSD/GPL (preserved).
   Linux 6.6 adaptation: congestion-control state stays in a heap allocation
   referenced from the 104-byte inline area, the existing cong_control(sk, rs)
   and skb_marked_lost callbacks are kept, TX_START/TLP stay in cwnd_event.
   Not ported: Google's BPF kfunc registration and callback ABI.
   Deliberate deviations from Google: the Linux 6.6 core's tcp_congestion_ops
   min_tso_segs callback is retained (Google's 2026 update no longer registers
   that callback, while retaining internal TSO/GSO sizing helpers), so a
   comparison against the core measures only the algorithm update;
   MODULE_VERSION reads "3-2026x" as a runtime marker (BBR_VERSION reported in
   tcp_info stays 3); the core's TCP_ECN_LOW and TCP_ECN_ECT_PERMANENT defines
   stay in place unused, so the patch applies to the v1.1 (value 3) and v1.2
   (value 32) core; BBRv3-specific ECN and PLB logic (including the ECN_LOW
   route feature) is removed, while generic TCP ECN and kernel PLB are untouched.
   SHA256: dcb798e9b9cb159273de3fb1d2eb0b731fb8cc2d4be0b2c8cd60bc63ebad8834

